        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "     1→---\n     2→name: nextjs-pages-router\n     3→description: PostHog integration for Next.js Pages Router applications\n     4→metadata:\n     5→  author: PostHog\n     6→  version: dev\n     7→---\n     8→\n     9→# PostHog integration for Next.js Pages Router\n    10→\n    11→This skill helps you add PostHog analytics to Next.js Pages Router applications.\n    12→\n    13→## Reference files\n    14→\n    15→- `EXAMPLE.md` - Next.js Pages Router example project code\n    16→- `next-js.md` - Next.js - docs\n    17→- `identify-users.md` - Identify users - docs\n    18→- `basic-integration-1.0-begin.md` - PostHog setup - begin\n    19→- `basic-integration-1.1-edit.md` - PostHog setup - edit\n    20→- `basic-integration-1.2-revise.md` - PostHog setup - revise\n    21→- `basic-integration-1.3-conclude.md` - PostHog setup - conclusion\n    22→\n    23→Review the example project first to understand the pattern, then consult the documentation for API details.\n    24→\n    25→## Key principles\n    26→\n    27→- **Environment variables**: Always use environment variables for PostHog keys. Never hardcode them.\n    28→- **Minimal changes**: Add PostHog code alongside existing integrations. Don't replace or restructure existing code.\n    29→- **Match the example**: Your implementation should follow the example project's patterns as closely as possible.\n    30→\n    31→## Framework guidelines\n    32→\n    33→- Never use useEffect() for analytics capture - it's brittle and causes errors\n    34→- Prefer event handlers or routing mechanisms to trigger analytics calls\n    35→- Add handlers where user actions occur rather than reacting to state changes\n    36→- Remember that source code is available in the node_modules directory\n    37→\n    38→## Identifying users\n    39→\n    40→Call `posthog.identify()` on the client side during login and signup events. Use form contents to identify users on submit. If server-side code exists, pass the client-side session and distinct ID using `X-POSTHOG-DISTINCT-ID` and `X-POSTHOG-SESSION-ID` headers to maintain correlation.\n    41→\n    42→## Error tracking\n    43→\n    44→Add PostHog error tracking to relevant files, particularly around critical user flows and API boundaries.\n    45→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
